---
import PageShell from '../../../components/PageShell.astro';
import VideoPlayer from '../../../components/VideoPlayer.astro';
import { docLinks } from '../../../data/doc-links';
---

<PageShell title="Curriculum Coordination and Broader Teaching Impact">
  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      My teaching impact at Doane extends beyond the students enrolled in the courses I personally teach.
      As Biology Curriculum Coordinator, I lead and contribute to the design, implementation, support, and
      continuous improvement of online Biology courses offered through OLA. I also support adjunct faculty
      from hiring, onboarding to assisting in all curriculum and content questions.
    </p>
    <p>
      When I joined Doane, there were fourteen online Biology courses. These courses had not consistently
      been developed through a process that involved the Biology Department in establishing their academic
      direction. One of my primary responsibilities was creating a process through which online courses
      could be aligned with departmental learning outcomes while also benefiting from the expertise of
      faculty who teach those disciplines.
    </p>
    <p>
      I pioneered and spearheaded the concept of a Biology <a href={docLinks.courseConsultant} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Course Consultant,</a> through which on-ground
      Biology faculty could contribute discipline-specific expertise to online course development without
      assuming responsibility for the entire course-development process. I presented this model to the
      Biology Department, and faculty members selected courses in their areas of expertise.
    </p>
    <p>
      I then led a team of Course Developers (who were adjunct faculty), Consultants, and Instructional
      Designers and scheduled development work according to their availability. Throughout the process, I
      mastered thorough and transparent communication with the Biology Department Chair and provided
      regular progress updates.
    </p>
    <p>
      The course development process also revealed recurring challenges for Course Developers, including
      tracking required assets, developing instructional videos, and creating meaningful assignments. In
      response, I developed a <a href={docLinks.courseDevelopmentGuideline} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Course Development Guideline and Checklist</a> to make expectations and
      deliverables more transparent. Because multiple course developments occur simultaneously, I also
      created a <a href={docLinks.courseDevelopmentTracking} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Course Development Tracking document</a> to monitor progress, outstanding tasks, and
      responsibilities.
    </p>
    <p>
      My role continues after a course is developed. I support adjunct faculty through onboarding, <a href={docLinks.courseReadinessCommunications} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Course
      Readiness communications</a>, troubleshooting, and course spot-checks. I also communicate with vendors
      to address issues that affect the student experience and gather feedback from adjunct faculty about
      course functionality and third-party platforms.
    </p>
    <p>
      Since OLA offers two self-paced formats of course delivery, I created two videos explaining how the
      5-week and 16-week self-paced courses work. These videos were meant initially to be added to our
      self-paced courses for enrolled students but they will now also be used for marketing and admissions
      purposes for better student communication.
    </p>

    <VideoPlayer
      src="/videos/self-paced-courses.mp4"
      title="Self-Paced Course Formats"
      description="Overview of the 5-week and 16-week self-paced course formats offered through the Open Learning Academy."
    />

    <VideoPlayer
      src="/videos/curriculum-overview.mp4"
      title="Biology Curriculum Overview"
      description="A comprehensive overview of the Biology curriculum coordination and development at Doane University."
    />
  </div>
</PageShell>
